WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:03.420
(inquisitive music)

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.966
- The cruise industry
is testing the waters

00:00:05.966 --> 00:00:08.990
in its return in the U.S.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.620
Cruise ships are currently
sailing at a lower capacity,

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.289
with many of these ships requiring
passengers to vaccinated.

00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:19.730
But even then, there have
been COVID-19 case detected

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.646
even among vaccinated passengers.

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:24.969
So the cruise industry has
lost billions of dollars

00:00:24.969 --> 00:00:27.517
since the pandemic forced these companies

00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:31.906
to cancel voyages for more
than a year in the U.S.

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:33.038
And during that time,

00:00:33.038 --> 00:00:35.609
they laid off thousands of employees

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:37.848
and curtailed marketing expenses

00:00:37.848 --> 00:00:40.240
in order to stay afloat.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.789
And for now, these operators

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:43.485
are slowly generating revenue

00:00:43.485 --> 00:00:47.579
as they take in their first passengers.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:50.162
(crowd cheers)

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.840
But they have also budgeted
hundreds of billions of dollars

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.990
in additional costs

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.840
for measures such as enhanced sanitation

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.566
and COVID-19 testing for crew.

00:01:01.566 --> 00:01:03.985
So the cruise industry
has faced some challenges

00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:06.266
as they return in the U.S.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.233
And the first that they all
need to tackle right away

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:12.212
is to regain customer confidence

00:01:12.212 --> 00:01:15.560
after having various
outbreaks on their ships

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.640
early in the pandemic,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.347
such as the Diamond
Princess outbreak in Japan.

00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:20.597
And to counter that,

00:01:20.597 --> 00:01:24.513
they would have to employ
all these sanitation measures

00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:25.946
and safety protocols

00:01:25.946 --> 00:01:29.334
in order to reassure that their
passengers that are cruising

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:30.530
are safe.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.068
(upbeat music)
(crew clapping)

00:01:32.068 --> 00:01:34.698
(crowd cheers)

00:01:34.698 --> 00:01:36.520
- We're back! Whoo!

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.719
- The cruise lines have been bound

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:40.932
to the set of guidelines
and recommendations

00:01:40.932 --> 00:01:45.120
laid out by the CDC since October 2020.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.451
And one way that they're able to sail

00:01:47.451 --> 00:01:49.961
is to ensure that most
of the people on board

00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:51.930
are vaccinated.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:52.806
So even though

00:01:52.806 --> 00:01:56.840
having at least 95% of
people on board vaccinated

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.267
is a quick way for the cruise lines

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:00.977
to resume sailings in the U.S.,

00:02:00.977 --> 00:02:02.367
it's not that straightforward

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:06.007
because in one of the states
where they operate, Florida,

00:02:06.007 --> 00:02:08.056
which is a major cruise hub,

00:02:08.056 --> 00:02:11.683
there is a law in place
that prohibits businesses

00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:16.240
from asking their patrons
for proof of vaccination

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.965
and these cruise lines
generally want their passengers

00:02:19.965 --> 00:02:21.720
to be vaccinated.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.905
As a result, this issue
has led to lawsuits

00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:27.900
involving the CDC, the State of Florida,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.607
and also one cruise operator.

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:31.610
Some key indicators of success

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.019
for cruise lines in the near term

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:39.019
is to avoid having massive
Coronavirus outbreaks on board

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.360
because that was one of the main reasons

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.206
why their sailings had to be halted

00:02:43.206 --> 00:02:45.470
early in the pandemic.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:46.828
The new Coronavirus variant

00:02:46.828 --> 00:02:50.999
ultimately poses a risk to
the broader hospitality sector

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:53.838
and that includes cruise lines.

00:02:53.838 --> 00:02:56.389
And their full effect has yet to be seen

00:02:56.389 --> 00:02:59.550
on these first few sailings.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.510
The timing for when cruise
operators could return

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.550
to pre-pandemic normalcy

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.200
is pretty uncertain.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.700
Although cruise executives

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.698
have repeatedly cited
pent-up demand for voyages.

00:03:10.698 --> 00:03:13.365
(crowd cheers)

